[0.018,"o","\u001b[H\r\u001b[2C\u001b[1B\u001b[48;5;237m\u001b[38;5;231mreported by user\u001b[20Gin testfl\u001b[30Gght\u001b[39m                      \r\u001b[4C\u001b[2B\u001b[38;5;231m\u001b[1mere \u001b[10Gre you hosting Actual?\r\u001b[2C\u001b[2B\u001b[22mNon\u001b[7G\u001b[39m                                         \r\u001b[2C\u001b[2B\u001b[38;5;231m\u001b[1mOperating System\u001b[22m\u001b[39m               \r\u001b[2C\u001b[2B\u001b[38;5;231mNone\u001b[39m                          \r\u001b[1B\u001b[49m\u001b[K\r\u001b[1B  \u001b[38;5;246mThought for \u001b[1m2s\u001b[22m \u001b[39m\u001b[K\r\u001b[1B\u001b[K\r\u001b[1B\u001b[38;5;231m●\u001b[39m I'll investigate the location request feature in the codebase.\u001b[K\r\u001b[1B\u001b[K\r\u001b[1B\u001b[38;5;246m●\u001b[39m Reading webview setup code\u001b[K\r\u001b[1B\u001b[38;5;246m  ⎿  \u001b[39m\u001b[2m\u001b[3mNo secure context hack. So does WKWebView consider capacitor://localhost secure? I \u001b[23m\u001b[22m\u001b[K\r\u001b[1B     \u001b[2m\u001b[3mbelieve WebKit has a rule: origins using \u001b[38;5;153mlocalhost\u001b[39m are potentially trustworthy \u001b[23m\u001b[22m\u001b[K\r\u001b[5C\u001b[1B\u001b[2m\u001b[3mregardless of scheme? Let's check the spec: - Origin is potentially trustworthy if \r\u001b[2C\u001b[1B\u001b[23m\u001b[22m   \u001b[2m\u001b[3mscheme is \"https\", \"wss\", \"file\", \"ftp\"? (ftp removed) - or scheme is \"http\" and host\r\u001b[5C\u001b[1Bmatches localhost. - or origin's scheme is registered. So \u001b[38;5;153mcapacitor://localhost\u001b[39m is \r\u001b[1B\u001b[23m\u001b[22m \u001b[3G   \u001b[2m\u001b[3mNOT potentially trustworthy per spec, because scheme isn't http. WebKit implements \r\u001b[5C\u001b[1Bthis spec. Hmm, then geolocation would be blocked in Capacitor iOS apps! Unless \r\u001b[1B\u001b[23m\u001b[22m \u001b[3G   \u001b[2m\u001b[3mWebKit doesn't enforce secure contexts for geolocation. Does WebKit enforce secure \r\u001b[1B\u001b[23m\u001b[22m     \u